================================================================================

EXTRA CAR SLOTS

================================================================================

PURPOSE

Extra Car Slots extend car randomization beyond the vanilla Stock and DC slots.
Players can add any number of extra specification rows and give each row the
same randomization criteria available to Stock/DC cars. The generated
configuration uses a separate `extraCars` array.

The implementation is intentionally staged in this order:

1. UI (JavaScript)
2. Generation (Rust)
3. Injection (C++)

CURRENT STATUS

The UI, Rust generation, and DLL injection work described here has been
implemented. The remaining work is focused on broader regression and runtime
validation.

UI DESIGN

The Configure view contains an `Extras specification` page. It uses the shared
car specification controls, so each extra row supports source pool, source
rating, source obtainability, target rating, target obtainability, and custom
unlock criteria just like a Stock/DC row.

The page remains useful when it has no rows: an `Add Car Slot` button is always
visible. Extra rows always receive sequential display IDs (`extra-1` through
`extra-N`); the list is renumbered after rows are added, removed, or loaded
from saved state. Each row has a remove button at the far-right end of the
row. The remove button uses the same compact, transparent X-button style used
by the Cup pages.

`makeDefaultCarSpec(id)` creates one row object. `makeDefaultCarsSpec(ids)` is
implemented as a mapping operation over that factory, keeping single-row and
multi-row defaults consistent.

The dynamic behavior is kept small inside `CarsSpecSection`:

- use the selected category's rows instead of a fixed Stock/DC list;
- add and remove rows for the dynamic Extra category;
- add the action column only for dynamic rows; and
- keep the action button as the final DOM child of the row.

The spec-page preset controls are dead code and remain hidden. Extra-slot
support does not add dynamic preset target handling.

STARTING CARS AND SLOT COUNTS

Extra rows can be starting cars. The maximum number of starting cars is the
number of enabled Stock slots plus enabled DC slots plus the number of Extra
rows. Starting-car lock/configuration logic therefore uses the global slot
range, with Extra slots following the enabled Stock and DC slots.

Changing the car randomization mode normalizes Extra rows using their global
slot indices, including Starting Car overrides. Base Game Distribution leaves
Extra-row rating and obtain attributes editable; values that were locked by the
previous mode or Starting Car configuration are reset when entering that mode.

The same total-slot calculation is used by the Car Pool Rating Distribution and
Target Rating Distribution tables. Their maximum is allowed to be zero when
there are no enabled Stock/DC slots and no Extra rows; when Extra rows exist,
they contribute to the maximum even if Stock/DC randomization is disabled.

RANDOMIZATION AND POOL SEMANTICS

An Extra row may randomize to a Stock/DC car. That is valid when the selected
car is not already present in the final randomized Stock/DC pool. Extra-car
candidate selection must therefore use the remaining-pool behavior rather than
rejecting every vanilla-folder candidate.

Validation should reject a direct vanilla-folder assignment to an Extra row
only when the corresponding Stock/DC randomization is disabled. This is the
same duplicate-protection rule used for assigning a disabled Stock car to a DC
slot; it should not prevent valid assignments when the vanilla car is already
being randomized and can be removed from the remaining pool.

Extra rows participate in duplicate specific-car warnings, fixed-rating slot
counts used by distribution validation, and the enabled-slot count used by the
Car Options settings. Distribution values are not silently rewritten when
Extra rows are added, removed, or edited; invalid combinations are reported by
validation and prevent generation.

CUP OPPONENTS

Players may select Extra slots and Extra cars as explicit cup opponents. Cup
opponent handling therefore has an `Extra` category and stable slot references
for Extra rows, in addition to concrete-car candidates where appropriate. The
normal runtime pool may also select loaded Extra cars.

DATA FLOW

The UI state and generation payload preserve:

    carsSpecState.extraCars: []

Existing presets are normalized with an empty `extraCars` array when they do
not contain the new field. The generation payload carries Extra rows alongside
`stockCars` and `dcCars`; no Rust or C++ behavior should be inferred from the
presence of the UI field alone.

RUST GENERATION WORK (COMPLETED)

The Rust model carries Extra rows and generation produces an `extraCars` result
array alongside `stockCars` and `dcCars`. Extra rows use the same criteria and
randomized properties as Stock/DC rows while coordinating all three pools.

The resolver sorts all rows by the number of candidates. Specific car requests
normally have one candidate and therefore resolve before broader slots. The
sort is stable, so equal candidate counts retain the configured Stock, DC,
Extra order. The same unused-car preference and fallback behavior used by
Stock/DC rows is applied to Extra rows as well. This allows an Extra row to use
a Stock/DC car when it remains available in the shared pool.

Validation rejects a direct vanilla-folder assignment to an Extra row when the
corresponding Stock/DC randomization is disabled, or when the same specific car
is requested elsewhere.

Extra slots are represented independently of their eventual RVGL runtime
indices. The generated configuration preserves Extra slot order in the
`extraCars` array, while the DLL maps a loaded Extra car by its stable internal
folder name rather than assuming `extraCars[0]`, `extraCars[1]`, and so on are
RVGL runtime indices. Stock and DC configuration continue to use their
vanilla-index lookups.

INJECTION WORK (COMPLETED)

`loadExtraCars` remains the switch controlling whether the DLL loads Extra
cars. The UI should pass the Extra configuration without trying to own this
runtime decision.

`ConfigData` now consumes and serializes the `extraCars` array. No special
`ConfigManager.cpp` parsing path is needed because it already deserializes the
root object through `ConfigData`.

`Hook_LoadVanillaCarPool` continues to load the Stock and DC cars. The custom
loader now behaves as follows:

- with `load_extra_cars` enabled, the original loader sees every custom-car
  directory;
- with it disabled and `extraCars` non-empty, the `DirScan_Next` detour skips
  unconfigured folders before RVGL reallocates the pool or loads their files;
- with it disabled and `extraCars` empty, the custom-loader hook exits early;
  and
- selected cars continue through RVGL's normal allocation, parsing, skin
  loading, checksum, ownership, and cleanup behavior.

The directory detour uses typed `DirScanState` and `DirEntry` structures. Ghidra
verified `DirScanState` as 272 bytes (`path[256]`, `vfsMountIndex`, padding, and
`pDirHandle`) and `DirEntry` as 264 bytes (unused header, `nameLen`, and
`name[256]`). `OSDirHandle` remains opaque because the DLL only stores its
pointer.

After the original custom loader finishes, RVGL has sorted the custom section
by `CarInfo::rating`, using display name as the tie-breaker. The DLL then moves
configured Extra cars into the order of the `extraCars` array. When all custom
cars are loaded, unconfigured cars remain after the configured Extras in their
existing relative order. The live RVGL pool is reordered before the DLL
snapshot is extended, so both use the same runtime indices.

`ApplyCarMods` is run for each newly loaded custom car. Extra configuration is
looked up by internal folder name, allowing rating and obtain overrides to be
applied after the custom pool has been reordered.

The relevant verified RVGL addresses are `LoadCustomCarPool` at `0043fac0`,
`DirScan_Next` at `00537250`, `DirScan_Open` at `00537120`, and
`DirScan_Close` at `005375a0`.

COMPLETED VALIDATION CHECKLIST

- The Extras page shows `Add Car Slot` with zero rows.
- Rows can be added, edited, and removed without affecting Stock/DC rows.
- Extra rows can be locked as starting cars, and the maximum reflects all
  enabled Stock/DC slots plus Extra rows.
- Starting Car validation checks both aggregate availability and each starting
  row's effective source pool, source rating, source obtain, and target obtain
  settings.
- Extra row IDs remain sequential from `extra-1` through `extra-N` after rows
  are added, removed, or loaded from saved state.
- Both rating-distribution tables use the same total-slot maximum, including
  the intentional zero maximum when the total is zero.
- Distribution values remain user-controlled when slot counts or spec rows
  change. Negative edits become zero and minimum/maximum pairs stay coupled,
  but fixed-rating conflicts, out-of-range values, and unsatisfiable sums are
  reported as validation issues instead of changing other settings.
- Specific cars and unchanged source/target ratings contribute to the fixed
  rating counts used when validating both distribution tables.
- A valid Stock/DC candidate can be assigned to an Extra row when it is absent
  from the final Stock/DC pool.
- Disabled Stock/DC vanilla-folder assignments to Extra rows are rejected.
- Extra slots and concrete Extra cars are available to cup opponent selection.
- Old presets load with `extraCars: []`.
- The combined UI and Rust test command passes: `npm test` from the `ui`
  directory runs the JavaScript validation suite and `cargo test` for the
  Tauri Rust crate.

REMAINING WORK: RUNTIME VALIDATION

- Verify the DLL at runtime with multiple custom folders and mixed
  Stock/DC/Extra assignments.
- Verify both custom-loading modes: load all custom cars, and load only the
  selected Extra cars.
- Verify Extra list order remains slot order even when native car ratings differ.
- Verify selected Extra cars receive rating, obtain, and custom-unlock behavior
  after pool reordering.
- Verify missing folders, duplicate resolved Extra folders, and Extra slots
  resolved to vanilla Stock/DC cars do not corrupt the pool or indices.
